
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now delivery — and there's a coupling worth surfacing, because the identity decision we just made constrains it.

  [1mWe have no email addresses.[0m Name-only identity means there's no channel to reach someone who isn't currently looking
  at the app. So:

  - [1mIn-app only[0m (a bell + a notifications list) — coherent with name-only identity, no external service, no
    deliverability concerns, no unsubscribe flow. Costs you reach: if someone never opens the app, they're never
    notified. Cheap to add other channels later, since they're additional consumers of the same notification records.
  - [1mIn-app + email[0m — real reach, but it re-opens the identity decision: we'd have to collect and verify addresses, which
    is most of the auth work we just deferred. Plus a mail provider, templates, and unsubscribe handling.
  - [1mWeb push[0m — reaches a closed tab without needing an address, so it fits name-only identity better than email does.
    Costs a service worker, VAPID keys, a permission prompt, and per-browser subscription records that go stale.

  Recommendation: [1min-app only[0m, with the notification stored as a durable record rather than a transient popup. The
  stored-record part is what matters architecturally — if notifications live in a table, then email and push are later
  additions that read from it, and nothing about today's design blocks them. A transient toast, by contrast, would be a
  dead end.

  On freshness, I'd start with [1mpolling[0m over SSE or WebSockets. It's a few lines, it works through every proxy, and the
  bell being up to ~30s stale is not a real problem for task changes. SSE is a contained swap later if it matters.
